  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nIn the wake of a cold front passage on Thursday morning, drier air \nsettles in briefly until the arrival of another mid-level trough and \nweak surface front on Friday which will be round one of precip \nchances into the first half of the coming weekend. Deep layer \nmoisture will be scant with REFS mean PWAT values in the 0.5-0.75\" \nrange and surface dew points will see little uptick in weak pre-\nfrontal southwest flow. This will lend to scattered, light showers \narriving early Friday morning from the northwest with highest \namounts confined to the lakeshore where a bit better moisture \nis picked up from the warmer lake waters. All told, the chance \nfor measurable precip reaches 40-80% (highest north) with low \nprobability south of I-70. Any chance for >0.1\" through Friday \nnight is confined to the north of Pittsburgh, and even the \nwetter scenario is only ~0.25\".\n\nThat trough then closes off into a mid-level low which ensembles \nspin around the Great Lakes through Saturday. Within it, cold \nadvection in the low levels will bring ensemble mean 850 mb temps to \n0 to -2C. This, coupled with broad ascent from the upper low, will \nmaintain primarily lake-enhanced showers through the day on Saturday \nnorth of Pittsburgh and some upslope showers in the ridges. \nElsewhere, a blanket of low clouds should dominate with moisture \npull off of the lake. Much cooler air will filter in as well, though \ncooler being used relatively as it'll really be closer to normal for \nthis time of year. With that said, most of the area is favored to \nsee a cool drizzle as even the colder solution for surface \ntemperatures remains above freezing. However, it the WV ridges, do \nthink that the thermal profiles will be supportive of some wet \nsnowflakes or cold drizzle. The big question there will be the depth \nof the moisture and whether or not it extends deep enough in the \ncolumn to produce snowflakes or saturation cuts off within the \nsupercooled liquid region warmer than -10C and would favor a cold \ndrizzle even with surface temps below freezing. This theoretically \ncould produce very thin patches of ice on cold, elevated surfaces, \nbefore sunrise but shouldn't be of any concern on the ground and \nroadways with warm antecedent ground temperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe early weekend trough departs on Sunday and surface high pressure \nestablishes across the Ohio Valley returning mostly dry weather\ninto next week with near normal temperatures. There still is a \nsolution dominated by a subset of GEPS and Euro ensemble members\nin which the surface high is slower to build, and a deeper \nshortwave trough slides through early on Monday and does return \nsome scattered showers to our area which would require an \nincrease to our current areawide PoPs. Regardless, ensembles \nconverge to better agreement that the high does become our \ndominant influence with dry weather on Tuesday. Arrival of some \nweak mid-level forcing by the middle to the end of the week \ncould spawn some lake effect showers, but uncertainty greatly \nincreases with potential solutions ranging from strong ridging \nto several shortwave troughs.",
